@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Imperfect-Information Games — Selection Map (you evaluate a belief)
' Synthesis: what replaces minimax when the true state is not observable
' Level:  1

actor "Human expert\nhidden state, maybe simultaneous" as H

component "Determinization\nsample K worlds, solve each" as DET <<host>>
component "Counterfactual regret\nCFR / CFR+" as CFR <<app>>
component "Search + learned value\nReBeL-style" as RB <<cloud>>
database  "Belief over states\nposterior, not a position" as BEL <<db>>

H ARROW_MAIN BEL : what can this side know?
BEL ARROW_MAIN DET : cheap, reuses a\nperfect-information solver
BEL ARROW_MAIN CFR : want an equilibrium policy\n(guaranteed only 2-player zero-sum)
BEL ARROW_OPTIONAL RB : scale beyond tabular

note bottom of BEL
  **The evaluated object changes.** With hidden state
  there is no single position to score — you hold a
  distribution, and a policy that reads the true state
  is unimplementable //at execution time//.
  ----
  Training on privileged state is fine and standard
  (centralised training, decentralised execution: a
  critic may see everything while the actor sees only
  its observation). The constraint binds the
  **deployed policy**, not the training signal — so
  "never show the model the hidden state" is too
  strong; "the acting policy must consume only what
  its side can know" is the real rule.
  ----
  **Cheapest first.** Determinization needs only a
  world sampler and an existing evaluator
  (""determinization.puml""); CFR converges toward
  equilibrium but wants an abstraction and many
  iterations (""counterfactual-regret-minimization.puml"").
  ----
  Simultaneous-move games belong here too: each
  decision is a matrix game, so there is no
  alternating tree for minimax to walk
  (""../game-search/"").
end note
@enduml
